    "title": "Artemis II Launch Time: How to Watch in Every Time Zone",
    "text": "The Artemis 2, properly called the Artemis II, launch date and time are almost here, and it's an exciting day in NASA's world. On Wednesday, April 1, the Artemis 2 will set off into outer space, and that's not a joke.So, what's the Artemis II launch date and time? Also, how do you watch the Artemis 2? Here's what you need to know to enjoy the action.Artemis II Launch Date and TimeThe Artemis II mission is set to take place on Wednesday, April 1, 2026, at 6:24 p.m. EDT (2224 GMT) from Kennedy Space Center, Florida, according to research from NASA. It has a two-hour launch window, so it could potentially have a liftoff anytime between 6:24 p.m. and 8:24 p.m. EDT. If it doesn't happen on April 1, the backup launch date is April 6.So, the launch will take place between 6:24 p.m. and 8:24 p.m. EDT; between 5:24 p.m. and 7:24 p.m. CDT; between 4:24 p.m. and 6:24 p.m. MDT; and between 3:24 p.m. and 5:24 p.m. PST. Over in Europe, that's 11:24 p.m.–1:24 a.m. BST and 12:24–2:24 a.m. CEST.Artemis II: How to WatchYou can watch full coverage of Artemis II on NASA's official YouTube channel. Broadcast coverage starts with live views and audio commentary of tanking operations at 7:45 a.m. on Wednesday, April 1.Who Is on the Artemis II Crew?The Artemis 2, properly called the Artemis II, crew features NASA astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, and Christina Koch, and CSA (Canadian Space Agency) astronaut Jeremy Hansen, according to NASA.\"The crewmates have spent the final countdown phase focused on readiness and technical verification, remaining in quarantine under strict health monitoring and completing medical checks to ensure fitness for launch,\" NASA shared in an official statement. \"They have been following a controlled sleep schedule and nutrition plan to maintain energy and hydration for launch, while continuing to receive regular updates on the rocket’s configuration and weather conditions from crew quarters.\"It's been more than 50 years since Americans have headed toward the moon, and the Artemis missions have to goal to set the groundwork to establish a moon base. Eventually, the mission is to go to Mars. \"The next era of exploration begins,\" NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman said in a post on X regarding the moment in history.So, will the mission actually happen as scheduled? \"The space agency has said there's an 80 percent chance of favorable weather conditions to launch Artemis II today,\" Live Science reported on Wednesday. \"Today's conditions are forecast to be the best of all the days in the current launch window.\"Live Science adds that, \"The April launch windows for Artemis II run from today through to Monday (April 1 to 6), with the potential for a launch on any of those days. After Monday, the next launch window is April 30.\"This story was originally published by Men's Journal on Apr 1, 2026, where it first appeared in the News section. Add Men's Journal as a Preferred Source by clicking here.",
